WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.790
ED GLAESER: America's urban decline first began during the 1950s.

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:06.820
The process of suburbanization, abetted by prosperity,

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:09.580
massive automobile ownership, and the highway system,

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:14.738
plowed along during this era, as did the deindustrialization of older cities.

00:00:14.738 --> 00:00:18.130
During the 1920s, industries had left cities for suburbs,

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:20.140
like Detroit's River Rouge.

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:24.550
But after World War II, industry was more likely to leave the north all together.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:30.220
The Taft Hartley Act of 1948 enabled some states to pass Right to Work laws which

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:34.350
made unionization far more difficult, and many southern states passed these laws.

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:37.260
The work of Tom Holmes compares manufacturing growth

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:41.720
after 1947 in counties right on the border of two states,

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:45.090
one of which has a right to work law, the other which is pro-union.

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:48.370
Holmes found that manufacturing grew far more in the pro-business

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:50.220
states than the pro union-states.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:54.690
And almost all of America's older, colder cities were in those pro-union states.

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:57.980
So the 1950s were a tough time for many of those cities.

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:03.726
Boston lost 100,000 people during the decade, a 13.7% drop in its population.

00:01:03.726 --> 00:01:07.969
Detroit's population shrank by 180,000 or 9.7%.

00:01:07.969 --> 00:01:11.508
Pittsburgh also lost more than a tenth of its population.

00:01:11.508 --> 00:01:16.889
But during the 1950s the population of New York City fell by only 1.4%,

00:01:16.889 --> 00:01:18.669
120,000 people.

00:01:18.669 --> 00:01:21.852
New York was already proving to be remarkably resilient.

00:01:21.852 --> 00:01:25.797
Over the longer haul of the ten most populous cities in 1950,

00:01:25.797 --> 00:01:29.590
8 of the 10 are at least 20% less populous today.

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:34.210
The two exceptions are Los Angeles and New York, both of which have grown.

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:38.430
New York's future looked enormously bright in the early 1960s.

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:40.935
This was the rea in which glistening glass skyscrapers,

00:01:40.935 --> 00:01:42.711
like Gorden Bunshaft's Lieberhaus and

00:01:42.711 --> 00:01:45.701
Mies van der Rohe's Secrum building were built along Park Avenue.

00:01:45.701 --> 00:01:47.876
The United Nations was growing in Turtle Bay,

00:01:47.876 --> 00:01:52.500
giving New Yorkers the feeling that they were citizens of the capital of the world.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.430
Broadway's musicals, the works of Rogers and

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:57.185
Hammerstein still helped define American culture.

00:01:57.185 --> 00:02:01.689
And Ed Sullivan reached out from Broadway into tens of millions of American homes

00:02:01.689 --> 00:02:03.975
every Sunday at eight.

00:02:03.975 --> 00:02:06.115
The Yankees dominated baseball, and

00:02:06.115 --> 00:02:10.055
in 1961 Roger Marris finally bested Babe Ruth's home run record.

00:02:10.055 --> 00:02:13.510
It was the age of Mad Men, and New York was on top of the world.

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:18.340
16 years later, New York was essentially bankrupt.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:24.220
The city's population fell from 7.8 million in 1960 to 7.1 million in 1980.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:26.700
By the deadly burning summer of 1977,

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:31.380
it seemed as if all that bright future was gone.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:31.930
What went wrong?

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:36.800
How did such bright urban promise dim so quickly?

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:38.800
We should start with the economics.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:42.870
The largest industrial cluster in America in the 1950s was not

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:48.170
automobile production in Detroit, it was garment production in New York.

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:52.330
As transportation costs plummeted, it became much cheaper to sew

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:55.560
ordinary garments in low-wage southern states than Manhattan.

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:58.220
It was easy to ship them.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:02.650
In later decades, it would be even cheaper to make them in China and Bangladesh.

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:06.800
In a few short years during the 1960s, the garment industry, which had been

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:12.890
a mainstay of New York City since the 1820s, shed hundreds of thousands of jobs.

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:17.220
This same story repeated itself throughout New York's once mighty manufacturing base.

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:20.000
New York City's Harbor still thriving when Elia Kazan and

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:25.070
Marlon Brando made On the Waterfront in 1954 was now made obsolete

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:28.010
by New Jersey's new Elizabeth Marine Terminal.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:30.320
The parent company of Domino Sugar Refining,

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:34.660
once the city's most important industry, decided to expand its facilities in Texas.

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:36.940
Rather than it's landmark Brooklyn factory.

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:40.010
In later years, other industries, most notably finance, would make up for

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:41.780
the lost manufacturing employment.

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:46.710
But between 1969 and 1976, New York lost about 600,000 jobs.

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:50.450
Social distress accompanied economic dislocation.

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:53.350
For centuries, New York city has attracted the world's poor,

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:57.200
the worlds huddled masses yearning to be free, as Emma Lazarus put it.

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:00.375
Ellis Island was the port of entry for millions of immigrants to America.

00:04:00.375 --> 00:04:02.289
And New York was also the port of entry for

00:04:02.289 --> 00:04:05.849
hundreds of thousands of African-Americans fleeing the Jim Crow south.

00:04:05.849 --> 00:04:10.699
Between 1940 and 1970, the number of African Americans in New York City

00:04:10.699 --> 00:04:13.500
rose from 460,000 to 1.7 million.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:17.620
New York has benefited enormously from this flow of talent.

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:20.920
The genius of the Harlem Renaissance lit up the city and the world.

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:23.982
Queens was blessed with the presence of Louis Armstrong, who was born

00:04:23.982 --> 00:04:26.190
in New Orleans, for almost 30 years.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:30.410
Yet the legacy of slavery meant that many African Americans were poor,

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:33.150
like past immigrants from Ireland and Eastern Europe.

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:37.690
By the 1960s, cities weren't willing to just accept urban poverty.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:40.550
Urban reformers like Jerome Cavanaugh in Michigan and

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:45.825
John Lindsay in New York tried to use city resources to make poverty less awful.

00:04:45.825 --> 00:04:50.093
A completely laudable aim, but one that's difficult sometimes to square with

00:04:50.093 --> 00:04:52.203
the limitations on what cities can do.

00:04:52.203 --> 00:04:55.744
Poverty also came into the spotlight because of New York City's rise in

00:04:55.744 --> 00:04:56.459
crime rates.

00:04:56.459 --> 00:04:59.453
In 1957, there were 304 murders.

00:04:59.453 --> 00:05:03.054
By 1961, that number had increased to 483.

00:05:03.054 --> 00:05:07.518
In 1967, the year of the summer of love and far less notably my birth on

00:05:07.518 --> 00:05:12.128
the island of Manhattan, the number of New York homicides reaches 746.

00:05:12.128 --> 00:05:14.909
By the time I was 5 in 1972,

00:05:14.909 --> 00:05:19.956
the number of homicides has doubled again to 1,691.

00:05:19.956 --> 00:05:24.160
The number of annual homicides would remain over 1,500 the rest of the decade.

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:29.753
And hit 1,814 in 1980, almost 6 times the figure in 1957.

00:05:29.753 --> 00:05:32.907
There were safe parts of the city when I was a kid, but

00:05:32.907 --> 00:05:36.230
they had shrunk into small amounts of walkable space.

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:41.570
And children who grew up in poverty also grew up looking over their shoulders,

00:05:41.570 --> 00:05:42.739
fearing robbery or worse.

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:48.230
Mayor Lindsay's fun city had become a city of fear.

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:52.440
Abandoned spaces like the South Bronx bespoke urban decay.

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:56.320
The graffiti that seemed to be everywhere radiated both urban chaos, but

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:59.400
also the creativity that still surged in the heart of the city.

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:01.640
Lindsay may not have been able to stop the crime wave, but

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:04.770
he does deserve the credit for helping to keep New York essentially riot free.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:09.080
In the tense hours after Martin Luther King's death when New York,

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:12.420
like much of urban America seemed on the edge of explosion,

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:14.370
Lindsey walked the streets of Harlem.

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:16.900
He was a calming, caring presence,

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:19.750
reminding African American New Yorkers that he was on their side.

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:22.625
And they largely believed him.

00:06:22.625 --> 00:06:26.440
Lindsay had flaws, but his commitment to racial justice could not be questioned.

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:31.590
Why did crime explode in New York from 1957 to 1972?

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:35.480
Crime is disproportionately an activity of young men and

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:40.800
the Baby Boom had given America and New York a lot of young men by the 1960s.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:44.050
But changing demographics can only explain a small amount,

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:47.560
maybe a fifth of the rise in crime found in New York and elsewhere.

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:50.270
Certainly, there was breakdown in police effectiveness.

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:53.350
Some experts have cited a loss in morale due to investigations of

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:56.650
police corruptions or malfeasance, but it's hard to quantify that theory.

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:00.670
I think that the best explanations are largely social.

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:04.560
The first line of defense against crime is not the police force, but

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:05.950
society as a whole.

00:07:05.950 --> 00:07:08.228
Parents who restrain their children's excesses,

00:07:08.228 --> 00:07:10.255
communities that keep a watch on the streets.

00:07:10.255 --> 00:07:14.602
The social organization that once brought some order to New York's neighborhoods

00:07:14.602 --> 00:07:17.469
seems to have weakened dramatically after 1957.

00:07:17.469 --> 00:07:21.351
The most famous example of this breakdown was the 1964 murder of Kitty Genovese

00:07:21.351 --> 00:07:22.400
in Queens.

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:25.870
The dying girl's cries for help were ignored by her neighbors.

00:07:27.170 --> 00:07:29.930
Even if this social hypothesis is correct,

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:32.410
we don't know why New York society changed.

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:36.650
Jane Jacobs, the great urbanist, would lay some blame on public housing projects,

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:41.620
which replaced the defensible space of old tenements with tall towers and

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:43.680
open, often empty space.

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:46.960
In the old neighborhoods, there would be eyes on the street as people looked out

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:49.000
their windows from short buildings.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:51.925
In the new towers, the streets were divorced from the apartments, and

00:07:51.925 --> 00:07:55.063
consequently, there were fewer people watching when someone was mugged.

00:07:55.063 --> 00:07:58.281
My own work with Bruce Sasido documents that street crime is higher in

00:07:58.281 --> 00:08:02.167
neighborhoods with taller buildings, even holding income constanta, although

00:08:02.167 --> 00:08:06.121
apartments are actually less likely to be burglarized than single-family homes.

00:08:07.827 --> 00:08:11.970
The city responded to poverty and rising crime by spending more.

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:14.470
Lindsay had been elected on a good government ticket and

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:18.850
immediately tried to reign in spending by getting tough with public sector unions.

00:08:18.850 --> 00:08:23.031
This silk stocking Republican didn't know the street brawl that he was going

00:08:23.031 --> 00:08:23.617
to start.

00:08:23.617 --> 00:08:28.431
Mike Quill, the tough as nails head of New York's transit workers,

00:08:28.431 --> 00:08:32.259
went to jail for leading a 12 day strike in 1966.

00:08:32.259 --> 00:08:35.660
Lindsay got an injunction ordering the subways to start rolling again.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:38.400
Lindsay knew how to work the courts.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:40.070
But Quill just ignored the injunction,

00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:42.750
saying that the judge can drop dead in his black robes.

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:45.100
I don't care if I rot in jail, I will not call off the strike.

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:50.380
Quill won, and his workers' wages rose by almost a dollar an hour.

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:53.750
City spending rose while the city's economy was sputtering.

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:57.120
The Lindsay Administration responded with accounting ledgered men.

00:08:57.120 --> 00:08:59.120
When Lindsay left office in 1973,

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:03.610
the new mayor, Abe Beam, former accountant himself, was left to pick up the pieces.

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:06.820
Beam couldn't do it, maybe no one could have.

00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:09.060
By 1975, New York was on the brink of bankruptcy.

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:13.670
Beam sought federal help But President Ford wasn't going to give cash without

00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:15.920
some promises that New York would reform.

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:19.270
Promises that Beam couldn't really make and certainly couldn't keep.

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:21.920
When Ford turned to him down,

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:26.370
the daily news headlines screamed Ford to City: Drop Dead.

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:28.570
Surely one of the most famous headlines in history.

00:09:28.570 --> 00:09:30.650
Ford didn't actually say those words, but

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:34.460
in those years it seemed like not just the White House, but history itself.

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:36.280
Was telling New York City to drop dead.

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:40.960
New York only avoided bankruptcy by turning to the state.

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:44.150
Governor Carey established the Municipal Assistance Corporation that would keep

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:48.080
New York City liquid, but would also take over fiscal control.

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:51.920
Proud New York had lost the ability to manage its own finances.

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:56.220
By the end of Beame's term the city seemed like it was in total chaos.

00:09:56.220 --> 00:10:01.090
TV Cameras during the 1977 World Series caught Bronx buildings burning.

00:10:01.090 --> 00:10:04.248
The Fire Department's ability to respond had been reduced by budget cuts.

00:10:04.248 --> 00:10:08.200
A Summer black-out set off a crime spree.

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:12.460
Which sharply contrasted with the 1966 blackout when the city had stayed safe.

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:15.310
The murderous Son of Sam terrorized the city.

00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:17.180
In the midst of the maelstrom,

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:20.710
Ed Koch managed to get himself elected mayor over the discredited Beame and

00:10:20.710 --> 00:10:24.620
the unforgettable Bella Abzug who once owned the liberal heart of the city.

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:26.890
Koch had also been a liberal lion.

00:10:26.890 --> 00:10:30.920
He became famous besting the last boss of Tammany Hall, Carmine DeSapio.

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:36.321
But as crime rates rose, Koch morphed into the get tough on crime candidate.

00:10:36.321 --> 00:10:38.310
1977 was his year.

00:10:38.310 --> 00:10:41.980
As New Yorks voters cast away traditional ethnic politics and

00:10:41.980 --> 00:10:46.210
cast away the liberal hopes of the 1960s, the cities madness needed to be managed

00:10:46.210 --> 00:10:48.310
and voters thought that Ed Koch was the man for the job.

